1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,560
Lesson 36: what's new in 1.7, matching, loops, stacks and tips

2
00:00:05,610 --> 00:00:28,539
YA-NEC course, lesson 36: more of what's new in 1.7. Calculators, HF, Quad, «multiband» type.

3
00:00:28,589 --> 00:00:41,408
Five bands on the same spreaders, from 20 to 10 metres. You choose the band fed in NEC and what to do with the driven loops of the other bands: open, closed or on their own line.

4
00:00:41,458 --> 00:00:53,054
«Tune and compare» brings the fed band to resonance with all the loops and compares it with the same band alone: here the perimeters change by 1.5 % and the F/B by 4.5 dB.

5
00:00:53,104 --> 00:01:04,654
Magnetic loop: now also rectangular and octagonal, with turns in series or in parallel, and coupling by Faraday loop, gamma, capacitive or ferrite transformer.

6
00:01:04,704 --> 00:01:14,587
Below, the bands covered by the variable capacitor: tuning capacitance, efficiency, bandwidth at SWR 2 and capacitor voltage at 100 W.

7
00:01:14,637 --> 00:01:31,412
Tools, Gamma match, with Healey's formulas: step-up ratio, rod Z0, gamma length and series capacitor.

8
00:01:31,462 --> 00:01:42,593
«Tune gamma and capacitor» uses NEC to find first the rod length, then the capacitor: here 105.6 cm and 81 pF, SWR 1.03.

9
00:01:42,643 --> 00:01:51,326
Stub matching, from IW2FND's article: single or double stub, shorted or open. The load can come from the open model.

10
00:01:51,376 --> 00:02:00,526
The Smith chart shows the load and the path along the line; the table gives stub distance and length for the two solutions.

11
00:02:00,576 --> 00:02:26,136
Noise/GT/Stack, Stack spacings VHF, with the 10 element Yagi: the calculation tries the spacings and proposes the best.

12
00:02:26,186 --> 00:02:33,203
New: you can click a row of the table. It turns orange, and that spacing is the chosen one.

13
00:02:33,253 --> 00:03:30,024
«Create the stack (chosen or proposed row)» builds the stack with the orange row: here 2.841 metres, 18.05 dBi.

14
00:03:30,074 --> 00:03:39,114
Patterns, Smith: the side with R below Z0 is shaded orange, so you see at once on which side the impedance lies.

15
00:03:39,164 --> 00:03:46,981
In Parameters, too, the SWR tells the two sides apart: solid line with R above Z0, dashed with R below.

16
00:03:47,031 --> 00:03:54,115
Last new item, the Tips tab: short cards from the articles cited in the Technical documents.

17
00:03:54,165 --> 00:04:00,381
Each card shows its source and buttons that open the right calculator or chart.

18
00:04:00,431 --> 00:04:07,046
End of lesson 36. Enjoy YA-NEC 1.7. 73 de IC8TEM.
